      "text": "To find the inverse of the composite function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we need to determine \\( f^{-1} \\). By definition, the inverse of a function \\( g \\) is a function \\( h \\) such that \\( g \\circ h = i \\) and \\( h \\circ g = i \\), where \\( i \\) is the identity function. In other words, \\( f^{-1} \\circ f = i \\) and \\( f \\circ f^{-1} = i \\).\n\nLet's start by expressing the identity function in terms of the functions involved. Suppose \\( p(x) \\), \\( q(x) \\), and \\( r(x) \\) are invertible functions. Then, we can write:\n\\[ f(x) = q \\circ p \\circ r(x). \\]\nWe need to find a function \\( f^{-1} \\) such that:\n\\[ f \\circ f^{-1} = i \\]\n\nTo find \\( f^{-1} \\), we need to reverse the order of the functions \\( q \\), \\( p \\), and \\( r \\). This means that we need to apply the inverse of \\( q \\) first, then the inverse of \\( p \\), and finally the inverse of \\( r \\). This can be expressed as:\n\\[ f^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\]\n\nLet's verify this by showing that \\( f \\circ f^{-1} = i \\):\n\\[ f \\circ f^{-1}(x) = (q \\circ p \\circ r) \\circ (r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1})(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ p \\circ r \\circ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ p \\circ (r \\circ r^{-1}) \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ p \\circ e \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ p \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ e \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = q \\circ q^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ = x \\]\nThus, \\( f \\circ f^{-1} = i \\), which confirms that \\( f^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\).\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for \\( f^{-1} \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]",
